{% doc %}
  blocks/_comparison-cell.liquid — private.

  One answer in one column: a claim, a tick, a cross, or the number that makes
  the point better than either.

  ## Why it is not `blocks/icon-with-text.liquid`

  
  Icon with text is a glyph beside a sentence and it has no idea of being one of
  a set. This one is a cell — its height is a track of the table's grid, its top
  edge carries the divider its column drew, and the thing it says is only
  meaningful next to whatever the same row says in the column beside it.

  ## Five kinds, one file

    Claim   the label down the left of the drawing
    Yes     the filled tick
    No      the outlined cross
    Value   a phrase in place of a mark — *114mg Sugar*, *Slow Release*
    Empty   a row this column has nothing to say about

  Yes and No are the same element with two palettes, and both palettes live on
  **Comparison table** rather than here: a table whose third tick is a different
  green from its second is a table that will be, and a merchant tuning the
  marks should be doing it once. Everything on this block under **The mark** is
  an override, blank by default, for the one cell that genuinely differs.

  ## The divider is drawn here and configured there

  `.comparison-cell + .comparison-cell` is the selector, so the line appears
  between cells and never above the first one or below the last — in a column
  with a header row the first cell is preceded by the header, which is not a
  cell, so the rule is right in both shapes without a modifier.

  It sits on the cell's border box rather than inside its padding, so changing
  the space around a claim never moves the line, and the line's inset is the
  column's left and right padding exactly as the drawing has it.

  ## What it does not say out loud

  A mark on its own announces as nothing. The static markup therefore ships
  visually hidden text — *Yes* / *No*, translated — and
  `assets/comparison-table.js` upgrades it to *Third-party lab tested: Yes* once
  it connects, because it knows which row the cell is in and Liquid does not:
  `block.blocks` does not exist and `forloop` does not reach inside
  `{% content_for 'blocks' %}`.

  Private — the leading underscore — because a cell outside a column is a tick
  in a box. It is addable inside **Comparison column** and nowhere else.
{% enddoc %}

{%- liquid
  assign s = block.settings

  assign kind = s.kind | default: 'text'

  assign has_mark = false
  if kind == 'check' or kind == 'cross' or kind == 'icon'
    assign has_mark = true
  endif

  comment
    Which palette the mark reads. Both are published by
    `blocks/_comparison-table.liquid` as two sets of custom properties with the
    same suffixes, so the cell picks a set by name rather than by branching over
    six properties twice.

    A custom icon takes the Yes palette, because an icon a merchant chose is a
    thing they are saying *about* their own column rather than a mark against
    it.
  endcomment
  assign palette = 'yes'
  if kind == 'cross'
    assign palette = 'no'
  endif

  comment
    Which glyph. The two marks have a fixed one — a tick is a tick — and only
    the custom kind reads a setting. `snippets/icon.liquid` renders an empty
    `svg` for a name it does not know, so the name is checked here rather than
    passed straight through.
  endcomment
  case kind
    when 'check'
      assign glyph = 'check'
    when 'cross'
      assign glyph = 'close'
    when 'icon'
      assign glyph = s.icon | default: 'check'
    else
      assign glyph = ''
  endcase

  comment
    ---- what it announces ----

    A default that says something is better than a default that says nothing:
    a tick with no text is announced as an empty group. The merchant can replace
    it, and the module replaces it again with the claim the cell answers.
  endcomment
  assign sr_text = s.sr_text | default: ''
  if sr_text == blank
    case kind
      when 'check'
        assign sr_text = 'sections.comparison_table.yes' | t
      when 'cross'
        assign sr_text = 'sections.comparison_table.no' | t
    endcase
  endif

  assign preset = s.preset | default: 'default'
  assign text_classes = 'comparison-cell__text block-text--' | append: preset
  if s.color == 'gradient'
    assign text_classes = text_classes | append: ' block-text--gradient'
  endif

  assign align = s.alignment | default: 'inherit'

  assign classes = 'comparison-cell comparison-cell--' | append: kind
  if align != 'inherit'
    assign classes = classes | append: ' comparison-cell--align-' | append: align
  endif
-%}

{%- capture cell_style -%}
  {%- render 'spacing', block: s, declarations: true -%}
  {%- if has_mark -%}
    {%- comment -%}
      Each property falls back through the table's palette to a hard default in
      `assets/base.css`, so a cell that overrides nothing emits nothing and the
      table stays the single place the marks are tuned.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    --ct-mark-size: var(--ct-mark-{{ palette }}-size);
    --ct-mark-size-mobile: var(--ct-mark-{{ palette }}-size-mobile);
    --ct-mark-fill: var(--ct-mark-{{ palette }}-fill);
    --ct-mark-ring-color: var(--ct-mark-{{ palette }}-ring-color);
    --ct-mark-ring-width: var(--ct-mark-{{ palette }}-ring-width);
    --ct-mark-color: var(--ct-mark-{{ palette }}-color);
    --ct-mark-icon-size: var(--ct-mark-{{ palette }}-icon-size);

    {%- if s.mark_size > 0 -%}--ct-mark-size: {{ s.mark_size }}px;{%- endif -%}
    {%- if s.icon_size > 0 -%}--ct-mark-icon-size: {{ s.icon_size }}px;{%- endif -%}
    {%- if s.mark_fill != blank -%}--ct-mark-fill: {{ s.mark_fill }};{%- endif -%}
    {%- if s.mark_ring_color != blank -%}--ct-mark-ring-color: {{ s.mark_ring_color }};{%- endif -%}
    {%- if s.mark_color != blank -%}--ct-mark-color: {{ s.mark_color }};{%- endif -%}
  {%- endif -%}
  {%- render 'block-typography', block: s -%}
{%- endcapture -%}

<div
  class="{{ classes }}"
  data-block-spacing
  data-comparison-cell
  data-comparison-kind="{{ kind }}"
  style="{{ cell_style | strip_newlines | strip }}"
  {{ block.shopify_attributes }}
>
  {%- if has_mark -%}
    {%- comment -%}
      `data-comparison-mark` is what the module re-labels. The hidden span is
      inside the mark rather than beside it so the two are announced as one
      thing, and `aria-hidden` on the `svg` comes from `snippets/icon.liquid` —
      an icon never carries the accessible name in this theme.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    <span class="comparison-cell__mark" data-comparison-mark>
      {%- comment -%}
        No `size:` is passed. The snippet would write `--icon-size` inline, and
        an inline property cannot be swapped at a breakpoint — the glyph is
        sized by `--ct-mark-icon-size` in `assets/base.css` instead, which falls
        through the table's palette and can still shrink on a phone.
      {%- endcomment -%}
      {% render 'icon', icon: glyph, class: 'comparison-cell__glyph' %}
      {%- if sr_text != blank -%}
        <span class="visually-hidden" data-comparison-answer>{{ sr_text | escape }}</span>
      {%- endif -%}
    </span>
  {%- elsif kind == 'text' and s.text != blank -%}
    <span class="{{ text_classes }}">{{ s.text | escape }}</span>
  {%- endif -%}
</div>

{%- comment -%}
  Every setting below is a control a merchant reaches in the editor, and the
  schema is one element's styling surface: splitting it to get under Theme
  Check's count of 40 would split the thing the settings style.
{%- endcomment -%}
